        <notes>
            <note>It is unclear whether Crowder was a copyright holder or a trade publisher. See the discussion in the </note>
            <note url="http://edmondhoyle.blogspot.com/2013/11/the-hoyle-copyright-in-hoyles-lifetime.html">essay</note>
            <note> "The Hoyle Copyright in Hoyle's Lifetime (revisited)".</note>
            <note rend="paragraph"/>
            <note>The single woodblock ornament in the book once belonged to Charles Ackers who died in 1759. Henry Baldwin carried on Ackers' business and printed a number of books for his cousin Richard. See C. Y. Ferdinand, “Richard Baldwin Junior, Bookseller” in </note>
            <note rend="italic">Studies in Bibliography, </note>
            <note>42:254-264 (1989). On that basis, I tentatively identify Henry Baldwin as the printer here. </note>
